//! lotus/stereo_paste_over.rye — ALES121: stereo_paste_over — the overwrite paste carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. ALES114 named `paste_over` — write the `src` samples
//! over the clip IN PLACE starting at `at`, so [at, at+src.len) becomes `src` and the length HOLDS — for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard
//! together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung carries paste_over into stereo the way ALES116
//! carried replace and ALES120 carried duplicate: run ALES114's proven mono `paste_over` on each channel over the SAME span, so punching a clean take over a flubbed bar lands on both
//! speakers at once. Where stereo_replace (ALES116) inserts a payload and can resize the channels, stereo_paste_over writes ON TOP and keeps the length — the length-preserving paste mode.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: stereo_paste_over(sc, at, left_src, right_src) writes left_src over [at, at+left_src.len) in the left channel and right_src over the same span in the right,
//! reusing ALES114's mono `paste_over` per channel over its public API. BOTH channels are validated BEFORE either is mutated — a stereo payload names ONE span across both channels, so
//! its two sides must be the same length (BadRange otherwise), and the mono preconditions are checked once against the shared length up front, so once they pass each mono paste_over is
//! pre-validated to succeed and a refusal never desynchronises the channels. It reuses ALES2's EditError whole and invents none of its own.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO OVERWRITE LAW: the left channel equals mono paste_over(left, at, left_src) and the right equals mono paste_over(right, at, right_src), each byte for
//! byte, every sample outside the span unchanged. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length — overwrite writes values only, never
//! resizing, so the stereo image stays aligned. THE INSERT-CONTRAST LAW: the same payloads at the same `at` keep the length here and grow both channels through ALES116's stereo_replace
//! with count 0 (the insert twin), the two paste modes side by side. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; empty
//! payloads the identity on both, mismatched payloads BadRange, a paste past the samples BadRange.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES121.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-021504_fill-ales121-lotus-stereo-paste-over.md
//! Stands on: lotus/paste_over.rye (ALES114) — the mono overwrite paste run once per channel; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance
//! invariant; lotus/stereo_replace.rye (ALES116) — the insert-mode stereo paste imported by the selftest to prove the insert-contrast law; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its
//! max_clip bound, and the EditError reused whole; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the peak/RMS meter, used only by the witness to read the result.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It writes caller-supplied samples over an existing span in
//! each channel — changing no length, inventing no sample, and reading no byte past either channel's len. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_paste_over.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const paste_over = @import("paste_over.rye");
const stereo_replace = @import("stereo_replace.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const stereo_paste_over_version = "20260815.021504";

/// stereo_paste_over forwards ALES2's OWN fault reused by name — BadRange (a write past the samples, or two payloads of unequal length naming inconsistent spans) — and invents none.
pub const StereoPasteOverError = timeline.EditError;

/// Write left_src over [at, at+left_src.len) in the left channel and right_src over the same span in the right, in lockstep — the stereo image staying balanced and the length holding.
/// Both channels are validated before either is mutated: the payloads must name the same span length (BadRange otherwise) and the mono preconditions are checked once against the shared
/// length up front, so a refusal never leaves one channel written and the other not. Reuses ALES114's mono paste_over per channel.
pub fn stereo_paste_over(sc: *pan.StereoClip, at: u32, left_src: []const i16, right_src: []const i16) StereoPasteOverError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: a stereo payload names ONE span across both channels, so its two sides must be the same length — an unequal pair names inconsistent spans, refused by name before any
    //            write so the channels can never desynchronise.
    if (left_src.len != right_src.len) return error.BadRange;

    // invariant: the payload never exceeds the whole timeline bound — bound it once against max_clip before it names a u32 size, the same guard ALES114 keeps per channel.
    if (left_src.len > timeline.max_clip) return error.BadRange;
    const count: u32 = @intCast(left_src.len);

    // invariant: the write lies wholly within the (shared) current samples, checked without underflow — count against the remainder — refused by name before any write.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (at > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - at) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono paste_over is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits.
    try paste_over.paste_over(&sc.left, at, left_src);
    try paste_over.paste_over(&sc.right, at, right_src);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — overwrite writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO OVERWRITE LAW — each channel equals ALES114's mono paste_over on that channel byte for byte, the length held; THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — left.len == right.len
//     after, both holding their starting length; THE INSERT-CONTRAST LAW — the same payloads keep the length here and grow both channels through ALES116's stereo_replace(count 0); THE
//     ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — a refusal leaves both channels untouched, empty payloads the identity, mismatched payloads BadRange ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO OVERWRITE LAW — each channel equals ALES114's mono paste_over on that channel, proven side by side over one anchor with different per-channel payloads.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try stereo_paste_over(&sc, 1, &[_]i16{ 77, 88 }, &[_]i16{ 78, 89 }); // overwrite [1,3) both channels

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try paste_over.paste_over(&ml, 1, &[_]i16{ 77, 88 });
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try paste_over.paste_over(&mr, 1, &[_]i16{ 78, 89 });

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // Concretely: {10,77,88,40,50} on the left, its per-channel twin on the right; length held at 5.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 77, 88, 40, 50 })) return error.LeftSpanWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 11, 78, 89, 41, 51 })) return error.RightSpanWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; the stereo image stays aligned. Proven at the head and through the tail.
    {
        var head = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&head, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8 });
        try stereo_paste_over(&head, 0, &[_]i16{99}, &[_]i16{-99}); // overwrite the head
        if (head.left.len != head.right.len) return error.HeadUnbalanced;
        if (head.left.len != 3) return error.HeadLength;
        if (!eq(head.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 99, 2, 3 })) return error.HeadLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(head.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -99, 7, 8 })) return error.HeadRightWrong;

        var tail = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&tail, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8 });
        try stereo_paste_over(&tail, 1, &[_]i16{ 55, 66 }, &[_]i16{ 44, 33 }); // through the tail
        if (tail.left.len != tail.right.len) return error.TailUnbalanced;
        if (!eq(tail.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 55, 66 })) return error.TailLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(tail.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 6, 44, 33 })) return error.TailRightWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE INSERT-CONTRAST LAW — the same payloads at the same `at` keep the length through stereo_paste_over and grow both channels by count through ALES116's stereo_replace(count 0).
    {
        const l0 = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const r0 = [_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41 };
        const lp = [_]i16{ 7, 8 };
        const rp = [_]i16{ 5, 6 };

        var over = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&over, &l0, &r0);
        try stereo_paste_over(&over, 1, &lp, &rp); // overwrite — length holds
        if (over.left.len != 4 or over.right.len != 4) return error.OverwriteGrewLength;
        if (!eq(over.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 7, 8, 40 })) return error.OverwriteLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(over.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 11, 5, 6, 41 })) return error.OverwriteRightWrong;

        var ins = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&ins, &l0, &r0);
        try stereo_replace.stereo_replace(&ins, 1, 0, &lp, &rp); // insert — both channels grow by count
        if (ins.left.len != 6 or ins.right.len != 6) return error.InsertLengthWrong;
        if (!eq(ins.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 7, 8, 20, 30, 40 })) return error.InsertLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(ins.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 11, 5, 6, 21, 31, 41 })) return error.InsertRightWrong;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW — a refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, proven for mismatched payloads and a paste past the samples.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 });

        if (stereo_paste_over(&sc, 0, &[_]i16{ 9, 9 }, &[_]i16{9})) |_| return error.MismatchNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (stereo_paste_over(&sc, 2, &[_]i16{ 9, 9, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 9, 9, 9 })) |_| return error.PastNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.RefusedWroteLeft;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 })) return error.RefusedWroteRight;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.RefusedUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE DEGENERATE LAW — empty payloads the identity on both channels.
    {
        var zero = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&zero, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        try stereo_paste_over(&zero, 1, &[_]i16{}, &[_]i16{}); // paint nothing — the identity
        if (!eq(zero.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.ZeroLeftMoved;
        if (!eq(zero.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.ZeroRightMoved;
    }

    _ = meter;

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-paste-over: STEREO_PASTE_OVER — the overwrite paste carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. Write left_src over [at, at+left_src.len) in the left " ++
        "channel and right_src over the same span in the right, reusing ALES114's mono paste_over per channel; a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that " ++
        "left and right hold the same length, so punching a clean take over a flubbed bar lands on both speakers at once. Where stereo_replace (ALES116) inserts a payload and can " ++
        "resize the channels, stereo_paste_over writes ON TOP and keeps the length — the length-preserving paste mode. BOTH channels are validated before either is mutated — a stereo " ++
        "payload names one span across both channels, so its two sides must be the same length (BadRange otherwise), and the mono preconditions are checked once against the shared " ++
        "length up front, so a refusal never desynchronises the channels. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO OVERWRITE LAW: each channel equals ALES114's mono paste_over on that channel " ++
        "byte for byte, every sample outside the span unchanged. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length. THE INSERT-CONTRAST " ++
        "LAW: the same payloads keep the length here and grow both channels through ALES116's stereo_replace(count 0). THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal leaves both channels " ++
        "byte for byte untouched and still balanced, empty payloads the identity on both, mismatched payloads BadRange. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips overwritten through the " ++
        "proven mono paste_over, reading no byte past either channel's len; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
